text:
jamie: quick sanity check before i open the PR — i'm adding a SecretBinding for the gateway to reference a cert in the infra namespace, and enabling TLS passthrough for the inference service. want to make sure health probes and targetPort wiring won't break our nodeport healthcheck. :thinking:

nina: can you paste the terraform snippet and the svc manifest? small differences in targetPort vs port have bitten us before. :eyes:

jamie: sure. terraform change is basically:```hcl
resource "Gateway" "inference-gw" {
  spec {
    listeners {
      protocol = "TLS_PASSTHROUGH"
      port     = 443
      tls { secretBinding = "infra/inference-tls" }
    }
  }
}
```svc is a NodePort backed headless-ish service for pods; containerPort is 8080, service.port=443, targetPort=8080, healthProbe uses http on /ready with port 15020 (sidecar) right now.

leo: wait — healthProbe on 15020 goes to the sidecar, but nodeport healthchecks from the NLB expect the service port (443) unless we set externalTrafficPolicy=Local. are we relying on NLB TCP healthchecks or ALB HTTP?

jamie: NLB w/ TCP healthchecks. We set externalTrafficPolicy=Cluster currently. so i think the NLB probe is hitting node and trying to tcp:443 which gets routed to kube-proxy -> pod targetPort 8080. sidecar probe on 15020 is internal only.

todd: if it's TLS passthrough, the NLB should be doing tcp healthchecks to the node:443. k8s forwards to pod:8080. as long as pod listens 8080 over TLS passthrough shouldn't affect the probe. problem is a readiness probe that is http on 15020 — the NLB tcp check won't catch app-level readiness and you can route traffic to pods that fail probe. i'd recommend either: 1) switch kube svc probe to target the app port (8080) with tcp/ssl check, or 2) keep current and add a gateway-level http-route healthcheck behind a terminator.

nina: other option: set externalTrafficPolicy=Local + use nodePort healthcheck to the sidecar port 15020 on node. BUT that requires nodes to have the sidecar port open and consistent. ops won't love it.

jamie: hmm. PR also includes SecretBinding change that references infra/inference-tls. terraform plan returned this snippet:```text
Plan: 0 to add, 1 to change, 0 to destroy.
~ gateway.inference-gw.tls.secretBinding: "infra/inference-tls" -> "infra/inference-tls" (forces replacement)
```not sure why replacement — secret binding is immutable in our controller?

leo: controller treats SecretBinding reference as part of listener identity; changing it forces gateway reconcile and may recreate the listener. that can cause ephemeral downtime if the provider deletes/creates the underlying LB listener. watch the rollout. add a canary listener if possible.

todd: also watch for certificate namespace RBAC — the Gateway controller needs get/watch on secrets in infra namespace. did you add the ClusterRoleBinding?

jamie: good call. i added this to TF for the controller role:```hcl
resource "kubernetes_cluster_role_binding" "gateway-secret-access" {
  metadata { name = "gateway-secret-access" }
  role_ref { kind = "ClusterRole" name = "gateway-secret-reader" api_group = "rbac.authorization.k8s.io" }
  subject { kind = "ServiceAccount" name = "gateway-controller" namespace = "gateway-system" }
}
```should be fine.

nina: sounds reasonable. only remaining risk is nodePort vs targetPort mismatch for NLB healthcheck. can you run a quick curl from a node to the pod 8080 and also try tcp connect to node:443 to confirm behavior? i can help run during the PR pipeline.

jamie: i'll run both now. from node-3:```bash
curl -vk https://10.42.1.23:8080/ready || true
# and
ss -tnlp | grep 443 || nc -zv <node-ip> 443 || true
```

deploy-bot: terraform plan succeeded — no drift beyond the listener replacement. plan URL: https://ci.internal/plan/1234 :white_check_mark:

leo: if plan looks clean, add a canary annotation on the gateway listener and flip on passthrough for a single AZ first. roll forward if metrics look good.

jamie: ok i did the node checks, curl returns 200 on pod:8080, and node:443 tcp connects and forwards to pod. i'll add externalTrafficPolicy=Local on the canary and a note in the PR describing the replacement risk + RBAC. thanks everyone — will tag for review in 10m. :thumbsup:
